                     ***[APPENDIX]***

[1]
Re: indexing, by Michael S. Hart:

My apologies to real indexers, as the naming and numbering
structure was not a high priority in the days previous; it
was originally considered that we would do one eBook 1971,
another in 1972, and then on to Shakespeare and the Bible,
with each play or book counting as one title, and thus our
numbering system for a while included about 40 files of an
eBook collection of Shakespeare that never appeared due to
changes in the copyright laws that were not completed when
Project Gutenberg went into action in 1971, and left us up
in the air for quite some time.  Please be advised that it
is again being proposed to lengthen copyright terms and to
return items from the Public Domain to copyright status, a
constitutionally unlawful act [ex post facto laws!] except
that treaties can somehow legally defy the constitution.

A THOROUGH reading of NAFTA, might explain that.

Thus, during the years when ONE play or book of the Bible,
or similarly sized file seemed like a major accomplishment
[remember, the FIRST Project Gutenberg eBook was only 5000
characters or so, The Declaration of Independence, but the
file took up 1/2000th of the entire space of the mainframe
we started on, which was two 5M hard drives.  Thus we said
we were not going to put all Shakespeare or the Bible on a
server at one time, since each of those files was 5 Meg].

However, unlike those who would prefer you to think a huge
amount of data is in their collections, we would prefer to
post Shakespeare and the Bible as a single file each, even
though they are each a thousand times larger than that 1st
file back in 1971.  With due respect to the United States,
and the spirit with which we started Project Gutenberg, we
will keep the first three file names as they appeared, WHEN
being the filename we used for the Declaration, BILL for a
copy of the Bill of Rights and CONST for the Constitution.


* * *
[2]
***Earlier Dates Are Approximations***
1980-1990 Various Editions of Shakespeare and The Bible

The Bible, Both Testaments, King James Version    [kjvxxxxx.xxx]   10

(The Bible and Shakespeare represented the entire effort for the 1980's,
(and the Bible alone is about 1,000 times larger than our first file,
(the U.S. Declaration of Independence, and so is the Complete Shakespeare.)

(Ed. note:  in 1976, U.S. Copyright laws changed significantly, but with
little notice to the general public; after a substantial amount of manual
typing, it was discovered the Shakespeare source being used was no longer in
the public domain.) (In 2023, based on the legal research leading to our "no
sweat of the brow copyright" how-to at www.gutenberg.org, the metadata for
all of Project Gutenberg's Shakespeare has been changed to indicate they are
confirmed to be in the public domain in the United States. This change was
based on them being transcriptions of printed works for which copyright has
expired, without any added creativity or authorship.)


