[3]
Re: the works of Sir Walter Scott, esp. the title Waverly"; also a quick
explanation of the entries in this listing.

The constraints of this GUTINDEX.ALL listing (lines of 80-character width)
sometimes require a sort-of shorthand"; many of the listings have titles that
may seem somewhat cryptic, which is why there are supplemental title, author and
information lines for many of the entries.

The works of Sir Walter Scott are an example of how this complication in listing
the works can be further complicated.  Waverly was first published by
Anonymous", and it was more than a couple of years before Sir Walter Scott was
acknowledged as the author; in the interim, he authored several more works,
which were attribted to the Author of Waverly".  Some of these were novels,
some were collections of stories.  All have become generally known as the
Waverly Novels, which makes them easy to confuse with the novel Waverly", the
first of those Waverly Novels.

(Ed. note:  may add list of Scott's works here)


* * *

[4]
There has been some confusion over the authorship of various publications of
the Boy Scouts.  The following is extracted from an email by William
Fishburne which may shed some light; this info may be updated as further
clarification becomes available.

The Boy Scouts on a Submarine,Captain John Blaine[bssubxxx.xxx] 6339
[Note: Not the same as #6108]

From: William Fishburne
To: Posted Etexts for Project Gutenberg <posted@listserv.unc.edu>
Date: Fri, 17 12:25:12 -0500
Subject: [posted] Re: Posted (#6339, Blaine)


--<cut>--
Apparently there was a huge effort to cash in on the boy scout
concept and there was limited protection on the name.  The number of
authors with boy scout titles is staggering because real authors
used multiple pseudonyms to protect their identity (and thus save
themselves from law suits).

This series was written by Robert Maitland and Georgia Roberts Durston
(aka George Durston, which was a pseudonym shared by several authors--
particularly used by J.W. Duffield when writing Boy Scouts with King
George which was subsequently reprinted as Boy Scout Aviators"--see
http://www.keeline.com/SC-A.pdf) according to
http://unofficial.umkc.edu/crossonm/boyscoutsdurstonmaitland.htm which
has been a fairly reliable source for this type of information for me
in the past.  This site also mentions that several of the books in the
series were reprinted under the name Edward Griggs."

It is worth noting that John Blaine is a fairly prolific author of
children's books and may well have contributed to this series.

The Library of Congress website says that John Blaine was a joint
pseudonym of Harold L. Goodwin and Peter J. Harkins.

The 1918 Saalfield edition is attributed to Georgia Roberts Durston
according the Library of Congress in keeping with your findings.
Apparently, there was also a 1921 printing from Saalfield attributed
to her.

The 1918 copyright is given in the cleared files as the year of the
edition attributed to Blaine.

There is another cleared version of this book with G. Harvey Ralphson
as the author (1912).  This 1912 edition was published by M.A. Donohoe
& Co (see http://unofficial.umkc.edu/crossonm/ralphsonboyscouts.htm).
It is apparently possible (I read about this on a couple of sites, but
didn't realize that I should have written the name down, sorry) that
this book was ghost written by Durston as several authors apparently
did the ghost writing for Ralphson.

Apparently she also used the pseudonym Colonel George Durston as well,
see http://www.seriesbooks.com/bseries.htm

It is probably safe to put Durston as the author with Blaine as a
pseudonym and it might be worth noting that she was probably a
ghostwriter for G. Harvey Ralphson (in the PG collection #6108).


